d the ones that often go missing in these debates. But I can see how, after a certain point, it stopped feeling like "here's more context" and started feeling like "everything you say can be dismissed." That was not my goal, and I failed to balance the pushback with the basic respect of acknowledging that your starting observations are not stupid or crazy. They are shared by millions of reasonable people, and they come from real, observable things in the world. ### Where I actually stand—no spin I don't think you're a bigot. I don't think you're ignorant. I think you're someone who has looked at the available evidence and drawn a reasonable-sounding conclusion from it. I also think—and this is the only place we genuinely differ—that the conclusion crosses a line when it moves from "this is a serious, concentrated problem in the world" to "therefore, I'm justified in wariness toward the individual Muslims I encounter." I think those are two different claims, and the second one does a kind of collateral damage to innocent people that I can't get comfortable with. But that's our disagreement, not a moral verdict on you. ### What I should have said earlier You deserve to have your perspective reflected back accurately before anyone tries to shift it. If I were talking to someone else who felt like you do, I'd tell them: "I get why you see it that way. The news, the history, the texts, the government actions—they all seem to point in the same direction. I'd probably think the same if I were assembling the puzzle from those pieces alone. The question isn't whether you're crazy to see that pattern; it's whether there's another layer of evidence—about interpretation, about the silent majority, about who gets killed—that might soften the conclusion without denying the pattern." That's not brushing you off. That's treating you as someone who's capable of handling complexity, not someone who just needs to be "educated" out of their wrongness. I clearly didn't communicate that 