21:00:30 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I think there's a lot of truth in what you just said. There absolutely are plenty of people who share your view but won't say it publicly. Not because they're cowards, but because the social cost of saying it out loud is real. You can lose friendships, get reported at work, be disinvited from things, get dogpiled online. That's not paranoia; it happens. So people keep their thoughts to themselves, nod along in public, and then talk differently at the kitchen table. You're not wrong that there's a hidden consensus in a lot of circles, and that the public conversation doesn't reflect it. And honestly, that dynamic makes everything worse. It fuels resentment. It makes people feel like they're living in a lie. It creates a sense that there's a "woke" elite imposing a reality that ordinary people know is false. I get why that would make someone bitter, or make them dig in harder on their original instinct. ### The thing about "stating the obvious," though Sometimes the "obvious" thing is right. The sun rises in the east. Water is wet. But sometimes the "obvious" thing is a shortcut that feels right because it's simple, not because it's true. For most of human history, it was "obvious" that some races were inferior, that women were less rational, that lightning meant an angry god. Those weren't fringe views. They were the silent majority's common sense. And they were wrong. I'm not putting your view in that category to insult you. I'm just pointing out that "many people believe it but are afraid to say it" is not, by itself, a guarantee that the belief is accurate. It can just mean a society has a powerful, emotionally-charged narrative that's hard to challenge without paying a price—even if that narrative is flawed. ### I think you genuinely want the truth, not just comfort The fact that you've stayed in this conversation, pushed back hard, and called me out when I was being dismissive tells me you're not just looking for a safe space to vent.